## Summary

Security audit of main at 134a42a (workflow stage "Security Audit · Sonnet high").

Read the docs and earlier Security findings (#28–#69, all closed), then went through the trust boundaries: auth/session/spoof, the public /hooks, /open and /ext routes, Mailgun and GitHub hook signature checks, mail intake (SPF/DKIM/ARC), MCP OAuth, bearer-token handling, image fetching, upload limits, URL scheme validation, blob serving (CSP sandbox), mail header composition, ICS output and frontend markdown. All held up except one.

Found: non-hosts could make the service mail unlimited invitations to any address. With a guest list's join=others, an outside guest (POST /open/ext/{token}/guest, token only) or a non-host member (/api/do/add-guests, /api/do/invite) could add guests, each mailed an invitation at once, with no bound (same class as #40 and #63).

Fix (policy chosen by ian via ask_human): each non-host can have at most 10 invitations mailed in their name per list (maxGuestInvitations), counted from RECIPIENT rows of invitation messages whose from_person is them, so taking a guest back doesn't free a place; hosts exempt. The check runs inside the write transaction (writeChecked in internal/db/writes.go, Invitations.writeInvitations) so parallel requests can't bypass it. Tests: TestAnOutsideGuestBringsOnlySoManyGuests, TestSimultaneousGuestsStayWithinTheLimit; docs/datamodel.md Guest lists updated.

Deliberately left: the chain (a brought guest gets their own token and own allowance of 10, so a determined attacker with several addresses can keep chaining). Reviewers flagged it twice; ian chose the per-person cap over "brought guests can't bring guests" (which would close it at a visible UX cost) and over a per-list pool. Also left alone: shared raw SESSION_KEY HMAC for unsubscribe/session/spoof tokens (no feasible cross-use; re-keying breaks old unsubscribe links), ICS param escaping of ':' '"' and lone CR (own copy only), Mailgun hooks parsing up to 40MB before signature check (flooding), tokens in request logs (#61 invalid), unauthenticated /csp-report logging.

Merged main into the branch with no conflicts; vet and the invitation tests pass.

