## Decisions

### Outside guests (and members) can make the service mail an invitation to any address, with no bound. How should a non-host's invitations be limited?

**What exists today.** An event, party or activity has a guest list. When its `join` column is `others`, a guest may bring more guests. Two routes do it and both mail the new guest an invitation (the "You're invited" email sent from Helios When) right away:

- `POST /open/ext/{token}/guest` (`extGuest` in `internal/db/invitehooks.go`). It needs no sign-in. The `{token}` is the `RECIPIENT.token` in the link of the invitation email, which an outside guest, or whoever the link was forwarded to, holds. Body: `{"name","email"}`.
- `POST /api/do/add-guests` (`addGuests` in `internal/db/invitations.go`). A signed-in member who is not a host calls it. `people` is an array with no length limit, and each entry is a directory person or a `{name,email}` from outside.

Neither route limits how many invitations one caller sends. `RECIPIENT` rows are the mail owed to each person; `invitationEdits` writes them.

**Concrete reproduction** (the flow `TestNewcomersAreInvitedOnlyWithAutoInvite` already tests): the event "Fondue Night" has `join=others`. The host invites outsider Coach Pat, who gets `/ext/<token>`. Anyone holding that token runs:

    POST /open/ext/<token>/guest  {"name":"Assistant Kim","email":"kim@example.net"}   -> 204, Kim is mailed "You're invited"
    POST /open/ext/<token>/guest  {"name":"x","email":"victim@example.org"}            -> 204, victim is mailed
    ... repeated with 500 different addresses                                          -> 500 invitations leave the HCA's Mailgun account

Each new guest gets their own `/ext/` token and can bring guests too, so the count grows by repetition. Deleting a guest (`DELETE .../guest`) and adding another does not reduce what was already sent. What the attacker gets: an anonymous way to send a real-looking Helios invitation to any address in bulk, spending Mailgun volume and sender reputation. This is the same class as closed findings #40 (mail to any address) and #63 (a member mailing anyone at will). The hosts' own sends are not the concern, only non-hosts.

**Recommendation** is the first option below. Which one do you want?

- (*) Cap invitations per non-host per list, count sent not current
  A non-host (an outside guest through the link, or a member through `add-guests`) can have at most **10** invitations mailed in their name for one list, counted from the `RECIPIENT` rows of `invitation` messages whose `from_person` is them. Hosts are exempt. The 11th returns 403 "you can bring at most 10 guests".
  Example, Fondue Night: Coach Pat adds Kim and nine more (10 mails); the 11th POST is refused with 403 and no mail. Pat deleting Kim and adding someone new is still refused, because 10 were already sent.
  Weakness: Kim can also bring 10 (the chain), so the total is 10 per person, not per list.
- ( ) Cap per non-host (10) and let only people a host or member invited bring guests
  Same cap as above, plus guests who were themselves brought by an outsider cannot bring more. Their `/ext/` page no longer shows "Add a guest" (`extView.Guests` false), and `extGuest` and `addGuests` refuse them.
  Example: Pat (invited by the host) brings Kim. Kim's page has no Add a guest button, and `POST /open/ext/<kim token>/guest` returns 403. The most a link can ever cause is 10 invitations from each person a host invited.
  Cost: a visible change, since a brought guest can no longer bring a plus-one of their own.
- ( ) Cap the list as a whole
  Non-hosts together get at most **50** invitations per list, counted the same way. Fondue Night: Pat's 10 and Kim's 10 and so on all draw from the same 50, and the 51st invitation from any non-host is refused.
  Cost: one abusive guest can use up the 50 and block honest guests from bringing anyone, until a host adds them.

The numbers 10 and 50 are my guesses at a party-sized limit; tell me if you want different ones.

ian@fc.run chose: Cap invitations per non-host per list, count sent not current
